n=by['E16-2609-055'];n['facts'][0]='The reported energy portfolio includes solar, solar–wind hybrid and compressed biogas projects; these have different operating characteristics. The previous storage-linked description was unsupported and is corrected.'
n=by['E16-2609-056'];n.update(block='02 · Urban infrastructure and public works',title='Amaravati CIIP cost revision: verify scope, rates and public service outcomes',why='The 23 September Cabinet report revises the Critical Infrastructure and Investment Plan for gram panchayats in Amaravati from ₹904 crore to ₹1,548.98 crore. This is not the Chintalapudi Irrigation Project; the earlier mistaken expansion is corrected.',facts=['The reported increase is ₹644.98 crore, approximately 71.35% over the cited baseline.','The revised reported scope includes 76.02 km of water-supply pipelines, 30.16 km of sewerage, 309.30 km of stormwater drainage and 69.68 km of roads, plus 2,597 streetlights and updated Schedule of Rates.','A revised administrative sanction differs from expenditure, tendered value, completed physical work and reliable public services. The original revised estimate and sanction were not inspected.'],static_link='CIIP here is an urban/capital-region infrastructure plan. Cost escalation can reflect scope, prices or delay; arithmetic alone cannot identify its cause.',analysis=['Publish component-level reasons, updated rates, remaining work, procurement milestones and service assumptions. Independent review should distinguish justified changes from avoidable delay or weak initial appraisal.','For APPSC, connect water, sewerage, drainage, roads and lighting to safe and inclusive capital-region development. Do not classify this sanction as irrigation spending or call the entire increase proven waste.'],trap='₹1,548.98 crore concerns Amaravati CIIP, not Chintalapudi irrigation; a 71.35% estimate increase does not prove a matching benefit increase or corruption.',mains='How can revised urban-infrastructure estimates be made transparent and accountable?')
n=by['E16-2609-057'];n.update(why='The 23 September Cabinet report ratifies two dated DA/DR instalments for employees and pensioners. The earlier interpretation as separate state-versus-central-pay categories is incorrect and is replaced; original finance orders remain unread.',facts=['The two reported instalments are 2.73% effective 1 July 2024 and 1.82% effective 1 January 2025, not merely alternative rates for separate pay groups.','Cash payments begin with September 2026 salaries according to the report. Arrears for July 2024 are scheduled in four instalments during 2027; January 2025 arrears in four instalments during 2028.','The report cites a state conversion formula of 0.910% for each 1% Union DA increase and estimates approximately ₹4,556 crore arrears liability. Employee-specific entitlement requires the operative finance order.'],analysis=['Clear payroll communication must distinguish accrual dates, current cash effect and deferred arrears. Both dated instalments are reported decisions; they should not be collapsed into one uniform immediate bank receipt.','For APPSC, include deferred liabilities and implementation capacity in fiscal analysis. The source is a Cabinet news report, not an audit of every salary or pension payment.'],trap='2.73% and 1.82% are two dated instalments in this report, not simply alternative pay-category rates; retrospective effect does not establish full September arrears payment.')
